      "A particularly important part of this approach was bringing state agencies and law enforcement into the same conversations as civil society and specialist anti-trafficking organisations. This helped reinforce a victim-centred approach to trafficking, where people experiencing exploitation are recognised first and foremost as victims in need of protection and support, rather than individuals to be criminalised or prosecuted for offences connected to their exploitation.",
      "That momentum carried into the World Cup campaign. On 18 May 2026, the Guadalajara campaign was formally launched at Tecnológico de Monterrey, Guadalajara campus, bringing together representatives from government, civil society and the private sector for discussions around trafficking prevention and the longer-term legacy of the World Cup.",
